The following files in the Nova source tree are ports of (and in places
near-verbatim adaptations of) the decimal-to-binary floating-point conversion
of the Rust standard library, library/core/src/num/imp/dec2flt/
(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust, commit
rust-lang/rust@48a229ceaefd4985c50990b14116b6d856af0985), which is itself a port of Daniel
Lemire's fast_float. Rust is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0; Nova takes it
under the MIT license reproduced below:

  - std/src/runtime/float_parse/convert.nv    (mod.rs, decimal.rs, lemire.rs,
                                               common.rs, slow.rs)
  - std/src/runtime/float_parse/pow5_table.nv (table.rs: POWER_OF_FIVE_128,
                                               re-derived by
                                               scripts/tools/gen-pow5-table.py,
                                               itself after src/etc/dec2flt_table.py)
  - std/src/runtime/float_parse/door.nv       (parse.rs: the assembly of the
                                               significand / exponent / many_digits
                                               triple inside Nova's own grammar walk)
  - std/src/runtime/float_parse/digit_buf.nv  (decimal_seq.rs: DecimalSeq as
                                               DigitBuf, parse_decimal_seq,
                                               number_of_digits_decimal_left_shift
                                               and both of its tables)

Types and primitives are adapted to Nova (the u128 product -> `u64
@widening_mul`, `leading_zeros` -> a library method, `[u8; 768]` -> `[768]u8`,
the generic `Float` trait -> one set of f64 constants); the algorithm, control
flow, constants, variable names and the comments that justify each rounding
decision are preserved from the Rust original. Each ported function carries an
in-file reference to its donor file.

